  "narrative": {
    "intro": "North Carolina has $3.5B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 62.1% year over year. The state shows 138,235 awards with an average award size of $25.6K. The Department of Defense is the single largest buyer, obligating $1.5B so far in the fiscal year. Other major buying agencies include the General Services Administration with $496M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $456M. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations at $317M, followed by Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $265M and Engineering Services at $215M. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. leads the vendor list with $174M, followed by SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC with $154M and RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE with $149M. Previous fiscal years show higher totals, including $9.3B in FY25 and $8.6B in FY22, indicating FY2026 to date is a partial, lower total. All figures reported are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial for the fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in North Carolina?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $1.5B in FY2026 to date, making it the largest buyer. The next largest buyers are the General Services Administration with $496M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $456M. Total obligations for the state are $3.5B, split across 138,235 awards with an average award size of $25.6K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors account for the bulk of contracts?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $317M in obligations, the largest industry category. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction and Engineering Services followed with $265M and $215M respectively. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is the largest vendor at $174M, with SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC and RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE at $154M and $149M."
      }
    ]
  },
